  "# A completeness gate that scanned the whole file

## What happened

`kotoba-lang/amu` keeps its test namespaces in two lists inside
`test/kotoba/compiler/test_runner.clj`: a `(:require ...)` vector, and a
separate `suite` vector. **Only the second is executed** — the runner reduces
over `suite`, requiring each namespace as it goes.

`test_runner_completeness_test` existed precisely to stop a namespace being
absent from one of them. Its own docstring says so, and names the day it was
written for: *Measured 2026-07-30: twelve namespaces were absent, four of them
RED. The four green ones were the harder case — tests sitting outside the gate
produce no symptom at all.*

It read the file like this:

```clojure
(re-seq #\"kotoba\\.compiler\\.[a-z0-9.-]+-test\" (slurp \"test/.../test_runner.clj\"))
```

A regex over the **whole file**. A namespace named in `:require` alone
satisfies it. The gate could not distinguish the two lists it was written to
compare.

## Measured 2026-09-06

- 171 test namespaces on disk
- 171 mentioned somewhere in the runner file — so the gate reported **0 missing
  and passed**
- **168 in the `suite` vector**

Three had never been executed under `clojure -M:test`:

| namespace | what it is |
|---|---|
| `kotoba.compiler.dom-app-driver-test` | the end-to-end test for the browser application driver |
| `kotoba.compiler.loader-source-classpath-test` | |
| `kotoba.compiler.dom-app-repaint-test` | added the same day, which is how this was found |

The first is the costly one. It is the test that pins the driver's fuel model —
one guest instance per interaction, 200 instantiations under load — and it was
outside the gate.

## How it was found

Not by reading the gate. By adding a test, watching the suite total stay at
1314, and asking why. The gate was green throughout.

## The fix

`region` locates each list by shape and `names-in` reads only that region, so
`:require` and `suite` are compared separately. The `suite` result is reported
first and by its own name, because that is the list whose absence produces no
symptom. If either region cannot be located the check **fails** rather than
degrading into a scan of nothing — a check that could not read its inputs must
not report a pass.

Break-tested: removing `dom-app-driver-test` from `suite` while leaving it in
`:require` fails with `test namespaces never executed -- absent from the suite
vector: (kotoba.compiler.dom-app-driver-test)`, and the run drops from 1321
tests to 1316. Before the fix that same edit was green.

## Two more instances from the same day, in my own work

Both were caught by running the check in the broken direction, not by reading
it. Neither would have been found by review.

1. A repaint assertion filtered for `data-k` values of the form `t<digit>`.
   The application numbers its items with **letters**, there being no
   integer-to-string builtin in the guest, so the filter matched nothing and
   the test stayed green with the optimisation it was written to measure
   switched off. Rewritten to name the list's own elements (`UL`, `LI`).
2. A break applied with a string replacement whose anchor did not match. The
   replacement returned the file unchanged, the run was not broken at all, and
   it reported green — which reads exactly like a successful demonstration that
   the check does not over-fire. The replacement now asserts it found its
   anchor before writing.

The second is the more dangerous shape, because **a break that did not apply
and a check that correctly declined to fire produce the same output.**

## Correction to the record

Two commit messages landed earlier the same day (`kotoba-lang/amu` PRs #823 and
#826) stated that their full-suite runs covered `dom_app_driver_test`. They did
not. The measurements in those messages stand; that sentence was wrong, and it
was wrong for the reason this ADR is about.

## The transferable part

This workspace already holds the rule (ADR-2608136000): a check that could not
measure must not return what a check that measured and found nothing returns.
This is that rule applied one level up — **the gate over the gates**. When a
runner lists its work by hand in more than one place, the check on it has to
read each place separately, and has to say which one a name is missing from.

A regex over a file answers the question *is this name written down anywhere*.
That was never the question."}]
